hey guys welcome to the third episode of  flashback Friday in today's episode I'll  be taking you through one of the most  popular and widely discussed  technologies machine learning but before  that make sure to subscribe to our  channel and hit the bell item to get  notified every time we upload a video in  a nutshell machine learning uses  mathematical algorithms to learn and  analyze data to make prediction and take  decisions in the future today  machine learning algorithms enable  computers to communicate with humans  autonomously drive cars write and  publish match reports predict natural  disasters or find terrorist suspects  machine learning has been one of the  most commonly heard buzzwords in the  recent past so let's jump in and examine  the origins of machine learning and some  of its recent milestones the concept of  machine learning came into picture in  1950 when Alan Turing a pioneering  computer scientist published an article  answering the question can machines  think he proposed the hypothesis stating  that machines that succeeded in  convincing human that it is not indeed a  machine would have achieved artificial  intelligence this was called the Turing  test in 1957 Frank Rosenblatt designed  first neural network for computers now  commonly called the perceptron model the  perceptron algorithm was designed to  classify the visual inputs categorizing  subjects into one of the two groups in  1959 Bernard Boudreau and Marcin Hawke  created two neural network models called  adilyn's that could detect binary  pattern and madelyn that could eliminate  echo on phone lines the latter had a  real-world application in 1967 the  nearest neighbor algorithm was written  that later allow computers to use very  basic pattern recognition Jarrell DeJong  in 1981 introduced the concept of  explanation based learning in which a  computer analyzes data and creates a  general rule to discuss unimportant  information during the 1990s work on  machine learning shifted from a  knowledge driven approach to a more  data-driven approach scientists began  creating programs for computer to  analyze large amounts of data and draw  conclusions or learn from the result now  let's talk about some of the  and achievements in this field in 2002  using a combination of machine learning  natural language processing and  information retrieval technique  IBM's Watson B to human champions in a  game of Jeopardy in 2016 google's  alphago program became the first  computer program to beat a professional  human using a combination of machine  learning and research techniques since  the start of the 21st century many  businesses ventured into created machine  learning projects Google brain alex net  d face deep mind open AI Amazon machine  learning platform and ResNet are some  large-scale projects taken up by  top-tier companies Amazon Netflix Google  Salesforce and IBM are dominating the IT  industry with machine learning machine  learning has came exponentially in the  recent decades as the quantities of data  we produce continue to grow so will our  computers ability to process and analyze  it so that is all for this episode of  flashback Friday like and share the  video if you find this interesting we  will be back with another technology in  the next episode until then keep  learning and stay tuned to simply learn  [Music]
 

